inclusion_criteria_en:
1、 Inclusion criteria for stroke
Inclusion criteria
(1) Meets the diagnostic criteria for stroke recovery in Western medicine;
(2) Meets the diagnostic criteria for meridians in traditional Chinese medicine for stroke;
(3) Meets the traditional Chinese medicine syndrome differentiation criteria for qi deficiency and blood stasis syndrome;
(4) The course of this illness: 14-180 days from the date of onset;
(5) Individuals with a history of cerebral infarction and no disabilities prior to this episode (modified Rankin scale score ≤ 1)
(6) Using the National Institutes of Health Stroke Scale (NIHSS) scoring criteria, the score for the degree of neurological deficit is ≥ 7 points and ≤ 22 points;
(7) Individuals aged ≥ 40 years old and ≤ 80 years old;
(8) Age sign informed consent form.
Note: If any of the above answers are "no", this subject cannot be enrolled.
2、 Inclusion criteria for dizziness
Inclusion criteria
(1) Age ≥ 18 years old;
(2) Feeling oneself or external scenery spinning, mild cases may stop with closed eyes, severe cases may be like riding a boat, spinning unpredictably, standing unsteadily, or accompanied by nausea, vomiting, sweating, tinnitus, deafness, and even falling down;
(3) The duration of symptoms varies from seconds to days, affecting normal life and work;
(4) Sign the informed consent form.
Note: If any of the above answers are "no", this subject cannot be enrolled.
3、 Inclusion criteria for dementia
Inclusion criteria
(1) Age ≥ 18 years old
(2) The main manifestation is a significant decline in cognitive function, especially in self comparison before and after, and a decrease in memory;
(3) Two or more cognitive impairments, such as orientation, attention, speech, visual spatial function, executive function, motor control, etc., whose severity has disrupted daily life and has been confirmed by neuropsychological testing;
(4) Corresponding lesions on CT and MRI.
Refer to the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, 4th edition (DSM-IV) dementia diagnostic criteria
Note: If any of the above answers are "no", this subject cannot be enrolled.
4、 Insomnia is included in the criteria
Inclusion criteria
(1) Age ≥ 18 years old;
(2) Difficulty falling asleep or having shallow nighttime sleep, easily waking up, having multiple dreams, or waking up 1-2 hours earlier than the average person and having difficulty falling back asleep after waking up. The next day, feeling tired, fatigued, or having insufficient energy during the day, making it difficult to engage in normal social activities;
(3) At least three times a week, the above-mentioned insomnia symptoms should occur and last for at least one month or more.
(4) Accompanied by significant anxiety, distress, anxiety, depression or fear, and leading to a decrease in mental activity efficiency, it hinders its social function.
(5) Exclude secondary insomnia caused by physical illness or other factors.
Refer to the diagnostic criteria for insomnia in the Chinese Classification and Diagnostic Criteria for Mental Disorders, 3rd edition (CCMD-3)
Note: If any of the above answers are "no", this subject cannot be enrolled.
5、 Inclusion criteria for diarrhea
1. Is it included in the criteria
(1) Age 18-70 years old;
(2) I have undergone colonoscopy examination in the past year and no abnormalities have been found;
(3) Informed and agreed to participate in this study;
(4) Recurrent abdominal pain, with an average of at least 1 symptom per week in the past 3 months, accompanied by 2 or more of the following: ① related to bowel movements. ② Accompanied by changes in defecation frequency. ③ Accompanied by changes in stool characteristics (appearance). And exclude other organic intestinal diseases.
The symptoms before diagnosis have been present for at least 6 months and have met the above criteria in the past 3 months.
(5) The proportion of loose stool (paste like stool) or watery stool is ≥ 25%, and the proportion of hard stool or block like stool is ＜ 25%.
Refer to the diagnostic criteria for diarrhea predominant irritable bowel syndrome in Rome IV functional gastrointestinal diseases;
Note: If any of the above answers are "no", this subject cannot be enrolled.

